## FIGURE 10: SpSAM (Integrated Omission Routing)

**Title**: "Spike-Spectral Attention Mechanism: How Hierarchy Responds to Omission"
**Status**: BLOCKED
**Scientific Question**: Which neurons/spectral modes jointly predict omission-sensitive state changes? How does hierarchy weight predictions?

### Invariants
- Integrate spiking + LFP spectral power (SpSAM mechanism)
- Compute attention weights per unit/frequency across hierarchy
- Compare omission vs. control SAM signatures
- Quantify lag relationships: Do spikes lead spectral changes (prediction) or follow (response)?

### Expected Result
- **Local SAM peaks**: V1 at p1/p2 (sensory prediction); PFC at p3/p4 (error response)
- **Lag signs**: V1 spikes lead alpha (predictive); PFC spikes lag theta (error-responsive)
- **Global SAM**: Hierarchy jointly weighs predictions; integrated signal stronger than individual areas

### Inputs
- Full-sequence epochs (SPK, LFP)
- SpSAM tensors (computed per [[spsam.skill]])
- Granger connectivity (hierarchy structure)

### Outputs
- `outputs/figures/f10_spsam_local.npy` — (n_areas, n_units, n_timepoints)
- `outputs/figures/f10_spsam_global_weighted.npy` — (n_timepoints,)
- `outputs/figures/f10_lag_heatmap.html` — Spike-spectral lag by unit/area
- `outputs/figures/f10_hierarchy_integration.html` — Multi-panel integrative figure

### Blocking Dependency
✗ `[[full-sequence-epoch-recovery]]` ✗ `[[spsam.skill]]` (both must be ACTIVE)

